## a b s t r a c t
The development of confined growth of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) in a nano-space remains a challenge mainly due to the spatial size randomness and inhomogeneity of host materials and the limitation of MOF species. In this study, we developed a general ‘‘stepwise vacuum evaporation” strategy, which allows the nano-confined growth of MOFs in hollow mesoporous silica nanospheres (HMSN) by the vacuum forces and the capillary effect. A series of nanoscale MOFs including ZIF-8, ZIF-90, HKUST1, MIL-53(Cr) and UiO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were confinely synthesized inside the cavities of HMSN, resulting in hierarchically porous composites with core-shell structures. Further functionalization was studied by anchoring Pd to obtain UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ Pd@HMSN catalyst, which exhibited excellent activity in the catalytic reduction of 4-nitrophenol to 4-aminophenol under ambient condition. $\circledcirc$ 2022 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
## 1. Introduction
Nanoconfinement effect means that when a substance is in a nanoconfined space, its physicochemical properties are significantly changed, such as density, solubility, reactivity and catalytic activity [1–3]. The concept of nanoconfinement first appeared in the field of catalysis and surface science, now it has become a research hotspot in many disciplines such as condensed matter physics, materials science, nanotechnology, biology and medicine [4–6]. Most of the materials currently used as the object of nanoconfinement effect are advanced functional materials, such as metal nanoparticles, polyoxometalates, metal halide perovskites, bio-enzymes, drug nanocrystals and metal borohydrides, which have the characteristics of small size, multi-active site, good dispersion and high stability [7–12]. Consequently, they are widely used in various fields, such as catalysis, sensing, drug delivery, gas storage and separation [7–12]. Therefore, continued investigation into the nano-confinement effect is of importance for scientific research and the application of novel nano-structured materials.
As a class of hybrid crystalline materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are made of metal nodes (metal ions or metal clusters) coordinated with organic ligands [13]. Featuring with ultra-high surface area, modularity and ease of functionalization, MOFs have become special porous materials for a wide range of applications [14–16]. Especially, compared with bulk MOFs, nanoscale MOFs (NMOFs) have higher specific surface area, which will expose more active sites and can effectively improve the sensing, electrochemical, drug delivery, therapeutic, etc [17–20]. In the meantime, NMOFs have favorable conditions for modification and host-guest assembly, which can enhance the performance of functionalized materials in catalytic reactions [21,22]. There are typically two synthetic processes utilized to produce NMOFs. One is to restrict supramolecular assembly. NMOFs are often created at nanoscale locations using emulsions or template synthesis. The alternative is to employ techniques like microwave synthesis, fast precipitation and ultrasound, all of which can promote nucleation to stop crystal formation [23]. However, some issues including unsatisfactory chemical stability and mechanical strength often affect the practical applications of NMOF catalysts [24–26]. In order to solve these problems, one effective strategy is to couple NMOFs into space-confined hosts to improve the stability [27,28]. Additionally, this strategy can exploit the synergistic effect between the two components to enhance the functions. For instance, Sohn et al. synthesized HKUST-1 inside the halloysite nanotubes. The obtained HKUST-1 nanocrystals exhibited enhanced stability to water and gas adsorption [29]. Yoo et al., reported the preparation of space-constrained HKUST-1 in nanoporous carbon matrices, which significantly improved the electrochemical long-term stability [30].
So far, only several host materials have been utilized for the confined synthesis of NMOFs including halloysite nanotubes [29], carbon-based materials [31–33], mesoporous polymer [34] and silica [35]. Among them, silica is of particular interest because the obtained $\mathsf{N M O F s@S i O_{2}}$ has the advantages of excellent structural tunability, biocompatibility and high chemical stability in practical applications [36]. In particular, the combination of mesoporous silica and NMOFs can realize the hierarchically porous composite material to enhance mass transfer ability. Therefore, NMOF ${\mathfrak{s o s i o}}_{2}$ composites are rapidly developed as new functional materials with broad application including multiphase catalysis, adsorption and separation [37,38]. Generally, the preparation of $\mathsf{N M O F s@S i O_{2}}$ can be divided into two strategies. The first one is ‘‘bottle-aroun d-ship”. In other words, the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ shell is assembled around the pre-synthesized NMOFs. It is noted that for the ‘‘bottle-around-sh ip” method, the NMOF structures may be destroyed during the harsh subsequent treatment, and the surfactants and template agents are not easily removed, which will seriously affect the subsequent reactivity of the composite material. The other strategy is known as ‘‘ship-in-a-bottle” method, which focuses on embedding NMOFs into pre-synthesized $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ porous structures. The metal and ligand precursors that make up NMOFs are introduced into the pores or cavities of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ by solution impregnation [27,39–41], vapor-assisted deposition [35,42,43], solid grinding [44] or homologous ion induction [45]. The desired confinement material is then synthesized by self-assembly under suitable conditions. Although the solution impregnation and solid grinding methods are simple to operate, NMOFs prefer to develop on the outside surface of porous materials rather than in restricted pores or channels, resulting in the production of physically hybrid composites [46]. In addition, the complexes synthetic process and high energy consumption of current vapor-assisted technique limit its practical application. Homologous ion-induced methods can produce well-dispersed confined NMOFs in cavities, but the generalizability is restricted by the requirement of ion source species and synthetic condition. In this situation, it is essential to create a technique for the preparation of $\mathsf{N M O F s@S i O_{2}}$ confined materials, that is not limited by NMOFs species and does not require tedious post-treatment.
Herein, we propose a universal ‘‘stepwise vacuum evaporation” strategy applying to ‘‘ship-in-a-bottle”. Space-confined growth of NMOFs in hollow mesoporous silica nanospheres (HMSN) was achieved to obtain NMOFs@HMSN core-shell composites. This strategy allows the synthesis of NMOFs in confined HMSN with high dispersion and small particle size. Further loading of Pd nanoparticles (NPs) was carried out to obtain Pd-based NMOFs@HMSN catalyst, which was studied for catalytic reduction of 4-nitrophenol (4-NP) to 4-aminophenol (4-AP).
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Preparation of NMOFs@HMSN via ‘‘stepwise vacuum evaporation” strategy
The strategy is comprehensively summarized in four steps: preparation of HMSN, stepwise vacuum evaporation of metal and ligand precursors into HMSN and thermal induction of the growth of NMOF crystals. As shown in Scheme 1, under the combined effect of vacuum force and capillary effect, the organic ligands and metal ions are added stepwise to pre-synthesized HMSN cavity, and then the precursors are uniformly locked in the cavity. During the secondary growth process, the triggering agent diffuses in the reactor at a suitable temperature. The high concentration of precursors in the cavity is rapidly transformed into a large number of NMOF crystals, which are preferentially confined in the HMSN cavity. To verify the universality of this strategy, a series of classical MOFs including ZIF-8, ZIF-90, HKUST-1, MIL-53, UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ were selected. Further functionalization was studied by anchoring Pd NPs to $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ by taking advantage of the binding effect of amino groups towards Pd to obtain Pd-based NMOFs@HMSN.
Preparation of HMSN. To $12\ \mathrm{\mL}$ of tetraethyl orthosilicate (TEOS) was gradually added $143~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol, $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water, and $6.4~\mathrm{mL}$ of ammonium hydroxide and stirred at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $3\mathrm{h}$ . Subsequently $3~\mathrm{mL}$ of octadecyltrimethoxysilane and $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of TEOS were added. Magnetic churning was kept going for another $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . The collected solid samples were impregnated in 0.6 M ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt C O}_{3}$ solution and stirred at $80^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ in order to form hollow structures. After the etching is complete, the product was centrifuged and rinsed several times with ethanol and deionized water. Finally, the product was calcined in a muffle furnace at $550~^{\circ}C$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ to eliminate the template agent.
Preparation of NMOFs@HMSN. In a round-bottom flask, $2.5\mathrm{mL}$ of an aqueous 2-methylimidazole solution $(24\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ was added to $0.3{\mathrm{~g~}}$ dried HMSN. After sonication for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the solution was rotary evaporated for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $40^{\circ}C$ until it totally evaporated. The solution was afterward added $3\ \mathrm{mL}$ of $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ aqueous solution $(24\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1})$ and rotary evaporated at $40~^{\circ}C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ until completely evaporated. The resultant composite product was after heated at $65~^{\circ}C$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ while receiving $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of triethylamine dropwise. The product was centrifuged after cooling to around room temperature and being repeatedly washed with ethanol and deionized water. Afterwards the product was dried at $85~^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain ZIF- $\mathbf{8}@$ HMSN.
For the preparation of ZIF-90@HMSN, HKUST-1@HMSN, MIL$\mathbf{53}(\mathbf{Cr})\ @\mathbf{HMSN}$ and UiO-66- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}@\mathbf{HMSN}$ , the procedures were similar as that of ZIF- $\mathbf{8}@$ HMSN except different MOF precursors and temperature were used. For ZIF- $\mathbf{90}@$ HMSN, $2.0\mathrm{mL}$ methanol solution of imidazole-2-carboxaldehyde $(19\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1})$ and $2.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of Zn $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ aqueous solution $\mathbf{\Sigma}^{'15\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1}},$ ) were added stepwise and each was rotary evaporated at $40~^{\circ}C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ until complete evaporation. Then, the resulting complex product was added dropwise with $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of DMF and heated at $85~^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . For HKUST1@HMSN, $2.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of 1,3,5-trimesic acid ethanol solution $(10\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1})$ and $2.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}$ aqueous solution $(25\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1})$ were added stepwise and rotary evaporated at $35~^{\circ}C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ until complete evaporation. Then, the resulting complex product was added dropwise with $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of DMF and heated at $85~^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . For MIL-53 $(\mathbf{Cr})\boldsymbol{\ @}$ HMSN, $3.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of disodium terephthalate aqueous solution $(15~\mathrm{~g~}\mathrm{~L~}^{-1})$ and $3.5~\mathrm{\mL}$ of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ ethanol solution $(24\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1})$ were added and the solution was rotary evaporated at $40^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ until complete evaporation. Then, the resulting complex product was added dropwise with ${80\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of DMF and ${80\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of sodium acetate trihydrate $(0.05~\mathrm{M})$ , heated at $180^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $15\mathrm{~h~}$ . For UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH_{2}@H M S N}$ , $3.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of 2-aminoterephthalic acid aqueous solution $(30\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1})$ and $3.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{{ZrOCl}}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{{H}}_{2}0$ ethanol solution $(40\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}) $ ) were added and the solution each was rotary evaporated at $40~^{\circ}C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ until complete evaporation. Then, the obtained composite product was added dropwise with $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of DMF and $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of glacial acetic acid, heated at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
Preparation of Pd-based catalysts. Typically, $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of the assynthesized UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH_{2}@H M S N}$ was redispersed in an aqueous ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{P d C l}}_{4}$ solution $20\ \mathrm{mL}$ , $0.5\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1}$ ). The mixture was subsequently stirred at room temperature for $24{\mathrm{~h}}.$ . After centrifuging and being five times cleaned with deionized water, the material was dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ overnight [47]. By reducing the final product with hydrogen for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $200^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{2}$ Pd@HMSN was produced. The Pd content is determined of $2.56~\mathrm{wt\%}$ by ICP-OES analysis. The $\mathbf{Pd}@\mathbf{UiO-66-NH_{2}}$ and Pd@HMSN catalysts with similar Pd content were prepared by the similar impregnation strategy for comparation of the catalytic performance.
## 2.2. Catalytic reduction of 4-NP
The catalyst dispersion $(50~\upmu\mathrm{L},1~\up g~\mathrm{L}^{-1}.$ was mixed with 4-NP $(2.0~\mathrm{\mL}$ , $0.15~\mathrm{\mM};$ , then freshly prepared aqueous $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ $(0.5\mathrm{\mL},0.25\mathrm{\M})$ was quickly injected [48]. UV–Vis spectrophotometer absorbance was used to calculate the amount of reactant 4-NP present throughout the reduction process. To average, all catalytic reduction tests were repeated three times in parallel under the identical conditions. In order to guarantee that the mass of the catalyst was appropriately separated in the circularity studies, the reaction was scaled up by 10 equivalents. Following the completion of the reaction, the catalyst was washed twice with deionized water, centrifuged, and ultimately dried in a vacuum drying oven before being used in the next cyclic catalytic reduction. At a room temperature of $25^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ , all catalytic reduction studies were carried out.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis and characterization
We have developed a general ‘‘stepwise vacuum evaporation” strategy for the synthesis of NMOFs@HMSN materials. This method exploits the vacuum forces and the transportability of HMSN, and can be extended to a variety of different typical NMOFs including ZIF-8, ZIF-90, HKUST-1, MIL-53(Cr), UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ by adjusting the MOF precursor species. In the ‘‘stepwise vacuum evaporation” process, both the ramping evaporation time and the concentration of MOF precursors have profound effects on the morphology and structure of NMOFs@HMSN. Insufficient evaporation time will lead low content of NMOFs in composite and isolated NMOF crystals. High concentration of precursors will result in the growth of NMOFs both inside and outside of HMSN.
The prepared HMSN shows a smooth and uniform spherical morphology with the average diameter of approximately $260~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 1a). The HRTEM image reveals the hollow structure features (Fig. 1b). The magnified HRTEM image gives the shell thickness of approximately $30\mathrm{nm}$ and clear ordered channels of HMSN (Fig. 1cd), which is highly accessible to chemical transport.
The morphologies and structures of NMOFs@HMSN were subsequently studied. As shown in Fig. 2, SEM images show a large number of crystalline clusters formed by MOF nanocrystals within the HMSN cavities. HRTEM images also demonstrate the uniform growth of NMOFs inside the HMSN, forming core-shell structures. Furthermore, the PXRD patterns of as-synthesized NMOFs@HMSN match well with the simulation results of corresponding MOF single crystal data, implying the successful preparation of these MOFs in the composites.
In order to study the functionalization potential of NMOFs@HMSN, we selected UiO-66- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}@\mathbf{HMSN}$ as the carrier to compound Pd NPs. UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH_{2}}/\mathbf{Pd}\ @\mathbf{HMSN}$ has a typical coreshell structure, as illustrated in Fig. 3a, and Pd deposition has no influence on the structural morphology of UiO-66- ${\bf\cdot N H_{2}}/{\bf P d}\ @{\bf H M S N}$ The HRTEM image shows a lattice spacing of $0.223\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is consistent with the crystal plane spacing of Pd (111) [49]. The average size of the Pd NPs is calculated to be approximately $5.07~\mathrm{nm}$ based on statistical analysis of the TEM image (Fig. 3c). EDX elemental mapping results show that Si is distributed in the HMSN shell of composite (Fig. 3d), while C, N, and Zr are tightly distributed in the UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ core (Fig. 3f-h), and Pd is mainly connected to the surface and gaps of the UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ crystal core (Fig. 3i).
PXRD investigations were tested to further study the structural properties of synthesized materials. The results reveal that the Pd deposition had no effect on the composite structure (Figure S1a). The additional diffraction peak at ${\sim}40.1^{\circ}$ is attributed to the (111) lattice pattern of the face-centered cubic palladium. These results suggest that the Pd NPs are successfully inserted and confined to the UiO-66 $\mathbf{-NH_{2}@H M S N}$ carriers.
The surface area and average pore size distribution of HMSN, UiO-66- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}@\mathbf{HMSN}$ , and UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH_{2}}/\mathbf{Pd}\ @$ HMSN were analyzed and studied by $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption test. The isotherm curves of $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption of HMSN show conventional IV curves with two hysteresis loops (Figure S1b). This shows that the material contains a large number of mesopores and macropores, which aid in the acceleration of metal and ligand precursors into the cavity [50,51]. Both the UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH_{2}@H M S N}$ and $\mathbf{UiO-G6-NH_{2}}/$ Pd@HMSN samples have hybrid of type I and IV isotherms. The presence of micropores emanating from UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ is suggested by the rapid rise in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption in the low-pressure region [27]. Meanwhile, high-pressure hysteresis indicates the presence of macropores in the composite materials. The BET surface area was reduced somewhat when Pd NPs are loaded, from $190.43~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ for UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH_{2}@H M S N}$ to $114.88~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ for UiO-66- $\mathbf{NH_{2}}/\mathbf{Pd}\textcircled{\pmb{\alpha}}$ HMSN (Table S1). Pore size analysis shows that the average pore size of $\mathrm{{\bfUiO-66-NH_{2}/P d}}\mathcal{@}{\mathrm{{\bfHMSN}}}$ is around $1.97\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is smaller than the average particle size of Pd NPs, therefore the loaded Pd NPs cannot be leached out. This facilitates the application of UiO-66- ${\bf\cdot N H_{2}}/{\bf P d}@{\bf H M S N}$ in multiphase catalysis.
XPS characterization was carried out to study the interaction between Pd NPs and the carrier. As shown in Fig. 4a,
UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH_{2}}/\mathbf{Pd}@\$ HMSN exhibits all of the characteristic peaks of UiO-66- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}@\mathbf{HMSN}$ , together with the peaks of Pd 3d. To further explore the valence and binding energy of Pd in $\mathbf{UiO-G6-NH_{2}}/$ Pd@HMSN, the fine spectrum of Pd 3d was analyzed. The doublet peaks corresponding Pd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Pd $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ are shown in Fig. 4b. The $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ peaks of ${\sf P d}^{0}$ is discovered at 333.3 and $341.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, and $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ peaks are at 335.7 and 347.2 eV [47,52]. The presence of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ may be due to incomplete reaction of the Pd precursor and oxidation in air [53]. Furthermore, the N 1s spectrum of UiO-66- $\mathbf{NH_{2}}/\mathbf{Pd}\textcircled{\pmb{\alpha}}$ HMSN has two peaks at 399.9 and $400.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 4d). The former corresponds to N-Pd, and the latter is attributed to the binding energy of the amino N-H bond, which is blue-shifted compared to that of UiO-66- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}@$ HMSN. This transformation further confirms that the $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ groups of UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ strongly interact with Pd NPs.
## 3.2. Catalytic reduction of 4-NP
Because of the high toxicity and carcinogenicity, as well as the hard degradability in water, 4-NP is regarded as a prevalent wastewater contaminant. However, its catalytic reduction product 4-AP is a vital organic chemical raw material that is frequently employed as an intermediary in the production of fine compounds such as dyes, medicines and pesticides [54–56]. The high activity and mild reaction conditions of noble metal catalysts on 4-NP reduction have attracted extensive attention of researchers [48,57,58].
As a result, we investigated the effectiveness of $\mathbf{UiO-G6-NH_{2}}/$ Pd@HMSN as a catalyst for 4-NP reduction in the presence of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ . As shown in Fig. 5a, the reaction occurrs quickly under the catalysis of UiO-66- $\mathbf{NH_{2}}/\mathbf{Pd}\ @\mathbf{HMSN}$ , and the absorption peak at $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ attributed to 4-NP decreases rapidly, which basically disappears after 7 min. In this study, the reaction can be characterized in terms of the pseudo-first-order kinetic equation $\ln(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{t}}/\$ $\mathsf{C}_{0})=\mathrm{ln}(\mathsf{A}_{\mathrm{t}}/\mathsf{A}_{0})=-\mathrm{kt}$ to describe the catalytic performance in the presence of excess $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ (Figure S2) [57]. As shown in Fig. 5b, there is a solid linear connection between ln $\left(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{t}}/\mathsf{C}_{0}\right)$ and time. The slope of a straight line can be used to indicate the reaction rate constant $(k)$ . As shown in Table S2, the reaction rate constants for UiO-66- $\mathbf{\nabla}\times\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{Pd}@\mathbf{HMSN}$ , $\mathbf{Pd}@\mathbf{UiO-G6-NH_{2}}$ , Pd@HMSN and UiO$\mathbf{66-NH_{2}@H M S N}$ correspond to $9.24\times10^{-3}$ , $5.83\times10^{-3}$ , $5.17\times10^{-3}$ and $4.72\times10^{-5}\:s^{-1}$ , respectively. Intuitively, the reaction rate constants demonstrate that among the four types of comparison materials, UiO-6 $\mathbf{\backslash6-NH_{2}/P d}@\mathbf{HMSN}$ has the quickest reduction rate of 4-NP. Another parameter for assessing the catalyst activity is turnover frequency (TOF). The calculated TOF value of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathbf{NH}_{2}\vert$ /Pd@HMSN is $213.8\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . It can be noted that UiO${\bf66-N H_{2}/P d}@{\bf H M S N}$ demonstrates excellent catalytic activity for the reduction of 4-NP when compared to the $k$ and TOF values of the previously published noble metal-based catalysts (Table S2). At the same time, UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{2}/$ Pd@HMSN achieves a comprehensive reduction of 4-NP within 7 min (Fig. 5c). With the same loading of Pd, $\mathbf{Pd}@\mathbf{UiO-G6-NH_{2}}$ and Pd@HMSN take 10 and $11\ \mathrm{min}$ respectively, to achieve almost complete conversion (Figure S3).
The catalytic activity and efficiency of UiO-66- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}/\mathbf{Pd}@$ HMSN are significantly better than those of the control materials $\mathbf{(Pd\@UiO–66–NH_{2}}$ and $\mathbf{Pd}@\mathbf{HMSN}.$ . Both the lower catalytic rate and activity of $\mathbf{Pd}@\mathbf{UiO-G6-NH_{2}}$ are due to that the exposed framework shows slight agglomeration and partially collapses during the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ reduction, thus leading to the aggregation of Pd NPs (Figure S4). For Pd@HMSN, it is because most of the Pd NPs are trapped in the hollow shell, which appear self-aggregation and disperse unevenly, leading to a decrease in catalytic activity (Figure S5). It is also worth mentioning that the content of ${\sf P d}^{0}$ in $\mathbf{UiO-66-NH_{2}}/$ Pd@HMSN is significantly higher than that of the other two comparison materials (Figure S6), also contributing for the high activity of the UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH_{2}}/\mathbf{Pd}\ @\mathbf{HMSN}$ material in the catalytic reduction of 4-NP. In addition, UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{2}/\mathbf{Pd}@\mathbf{Si}0_{2}$ was synthesized by conventional method according to the modified literature procedure [59]. The catalyst required 16 min to achieve complete degradation of 4-NP with lower reaction rate constant $(3.48\times10^{-3}\ s^{-1})$ and TOF $(81.4\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ ） as compared with title catalyst (Figure S7), revealing the promising potential of the confined synthetic materials for catalytic applications.
The catalyst recyclability was also investigated (Fig. 5d). UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{2}/\mathbf{Pd}@$ HMSN catalyst can be reused at least 10 times almost without degradation catalytic activity. Notably, the HRTEM image after 10 cycles shows that the core-shell structure is preserved with the same size (Figure S8a). In addition, the PXRD test results also demonstrate that the space-confined material maintained crystalline stability after being used 10 times (Figure S8b).
## 3.3. Catalytic mechanism
The reduction process of 4-NP using UiO-66-NH2/Pd@HMSN catalyst is proposed in Fig. 6. The catalytic mechanism is that $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ is first converted to $\mathsf{B O}_{2}^{-}$ and active hydrogen by hydrolysis reaction, and the generated active hydrogen is adsorbed on the surface of Pd NPs (Eq. (1)). At the same time, the reactant 4-NP is adsorbed onto the loaded Pd NPs and is finally rapidly converted into 4-AP after three consecutive reduction steps. Eventually, the reaction product 4-AP is desorbed from UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH_{2}}/\mathbf{Pd}@\mathbf{HMSN}$ and diffuses into water (Eq. (2)). The multi-level pore structure of HMSN enhances fast transport and diffusion of reactants and products during the catalytic reaction utilizing UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH_{2}}I$ Pd@HMSN as the catalyst, which is advantageous for multiphase catalysis. The interlayer voids can enhance the confinement effect, which allows chemical reactants with greater instantaneous concentrations to easily access the active sites, resulting in a driving force to a faster reaction rate. More importantly, UiO-66- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ nanocrystals provide attachment sites for Pd, allowing Pd NPs to be well distributed within the UiO-66- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}@\mathbf{HMSN}$ structure. At the same time, the effective physical-barrier effect can prohibit the confined Pd NPs from aggregation and detachment, thus, exposing more active sites to accelerate the reaction.
## 4. Conclusion
In this study, we propose a general ‘‘stepwise vacuum evaporation” strategy to achieve the confined growth of NMOF crystals, which uses the negative vacuum pressure and the capillary effect of HMSN to load MOF precursors into the cavity in a stepwise manner. The obtained NMOFs@HMSN can be further functionalized by the deposition of noble metal nanocatalyst. Owing to its unique nanoconfined environment and structural features, Pd based catalyst exhibits enhanced activity and stability in catalytic reduction experiments. This method is expected to provide a new avenue for the ‘‘ship-in-a-bottle” design and synthesis of other hostguest nanomaterials for applications in other fields.
